<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#An%20element%20in%20V%20is%20said%20to%20be%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20if%20it%20is%20orthogonal%20to%20every%20element%20of%20S.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#An%20element%20in%20V%20is%20said%20to%20be%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20if%20it%20is%20orthogonal%20to%20every%20element%20of%20S.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "An element in V is said to be orthogonal to S if it is orthogonal to every element of S." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#First%20we%20prove%20that%20an%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20actually%20exists.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#First%20we%20prove%20that%20an%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20actually%20exists.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "First we prove that an orthogonal decomposition (15.16) actually exists." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#If%20S%20is%20a%20plane%20through%20the%20origin%2C%20as%20shown%20in%20Figure%2015.2%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20line%20through%20the%20origin%20perpendicular%20to%20this%20plane.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#If%20S%20is%20a%20plane%20through%20the%20origin%2C%20as%20shown%20in%20Figure%2015.2%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20line%20through%20the%20origin%20perpendicular%20to%20this%20plane.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "If S is a plane through the origin, as shown in Figure 15.2, then $S^{\\perp}$ is a line through the origin perpendicular to this plane." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#In%20case%20S%20is%20a%20subspace%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20called%20the%20orthogonal%20complement%20of%20S.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#In%20case%20S%20is%20a%20subspace%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20called%20the%20orthogonal%20complement%20of%20S.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "In case S is a subspace, then $S^{\\perp}$ is called the orthogonal complement of S." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#It%20is%20a%20simple%20exercise%20to%20verify%20that%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20subspace%20of%20V%2C%20whether%20or%20not%20S%20itself%20is%20one.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#It%20is%20a%20simple%20exercise%20to%20verify%20that%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20subspace%20of%20V%2C%20whether%20or%20not%20S%20itself%20is%20one.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "It is a simple exercise to verify that $S^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of V, whether or not S itself is one." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Let%20%24S%24%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20%24V%24%20%2C%20and%20let%20%24%5C%7Be_1%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20e_n%5C%7D%24%20be%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20for%20%24S%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Let%20%24S%24%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20%24V%24%20%2C%20and%20let%20%24%5C%7Be_1%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20e_n%5C%7D%24%20be%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20for%20%24S%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Let $S$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of a Euclidean space $V$ , and let $\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_n\\}$ be an orthonormal basis for $S$ ." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Let%20S%20be%20a%20subset%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20V.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Let%20S%20be%20a%20subset%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20V.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Let S be a subset of a Euclidean space V." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Moreover%2C%20the%20norm%20of%20%24x%24%20is%20given%20by%20the%20Pythagorean%20formula> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Moreover%2C%20the%20norm%20of%20%24x%24%20is%20given%20by%20the%20Pythagorean%20formula> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Moreover, the norm of $x$ is given by the Pythagorean formula" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Next%20we%20prove%20that%20the%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20is%20unique.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Next%20we%20prove%20that%20the%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20is%20unique.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Next we prove that the orthogonal decomposition (15.16) is unique." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#THEOREM%2015.15.%20ORTHOGONAL%20DECOMPOSITION%20THEOREM.%20Let%20V%20be%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20and%20let%20S%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20V.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#THEOREM%2015.15.%20ORTHOGONAL%20DECOMPOSITION%20THEOREM.%20Let%20V%20be%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20and%20let%20S%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20V.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "THEOREM 15.15. ORTHOGONAL DECOMPOSITION THEOREM. Let V be a Euclidean space and let S be a finite-dimensional subspace of V." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#The%20distance%20between%20two%20elements%20x%20and%20y%20is%20defined%20to%20be%20the%20norm%20%24%5C%7Cx%20-%20y%5C%7C%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#The%20distance%20between%20two%20elements%20x%20and%20y%20is%20defined%20to%20be%20the%20norm%20%24%5C%7Cx%20-%20y%5C%7C%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm $\\|x - y\\|$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#The%20set%20of%20all%20elements%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20is%20denoted%20by%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20and%20is%20called> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#The%20set%20of%20all%20elements%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20is%20denoted%20by%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20and%20is%20called> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "The set of all elements orthogonal to S is denoted by $S^{\\perp}$ and is called" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Then%20every%20element%20x%20in%20V%20can%20be%20represented%20uniquely%20as%20a%20sum%20of%20two%20elements%2C%20one%20in%20S%20and%20one%20in%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#Then%20every%20element%20x%20in%20V%20can%20be%20represented%20uniquely%20as%20a%20sum%20of%20two%20elements%2C%20one%20in%20S%20and%20one%20in%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "Then every element x in V can be represented uniquely as a sum of two elements, one in S and one in $S^{\\perp}$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#We%20prove%20next%20that%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20%24S%24%20is%20the%20solution%20to%20the%20approximation%20problem%20stated%20at%20the%20beginning%20of%20this%20section.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#We%20prove%20next%20that%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20%24S%24%20is%20the%20solution%20to%20the%20approximation%20problem%20stated%20at%20the%20beginning%20of%20this%20section.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "We prove next that the projection of $x$ on $S$ is the solution to the approximation problem stated at the beginning of this section." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#We%20wish%20to%20consider%20the%20following%20type%20of%20approximation%20problem%3A%20Given%20an%20element%20x%20in%20V%2C%20to%20determine%20an%20element%20in%20S%20whose%20distance%20from%20x%20is%20as%20small%20as%20possible.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#We%20wish%20to%20consider%20the%20following%20type%20of%20approximation%20problem%3A%20Given%20an%20element%20x%20in%20V%2C%20to%20determine%20an%20element%20in%20S%20whose%20distance%20from%20x%20is%20as%20small%20as%20possible.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "We wish to consider the following type of approximation problem: Given an element x in V, to determine an element in S whose distance from x is as small as possible." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20the%20subspace%20%24S%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20the%20subspace%20%24S%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "is called the projection of $x$ on the subspace $S$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#the%20remaining%20terms%20being%20zero%20since%20%24s%24%20and%20%24s%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20are%20orthogonal.%20This%20proves%20%2815.17%29.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.14.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.14.md#the%20remaining%20terms%20being%20zero%20since%20%24s%24%20and%20%24s%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20are%20orthogonal.%20This%20proves%20%2815.17%29.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "the remaining terms being zero since $s$ and $s^{\\perp}$ are orthogonal. This proves (15.17)." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "approximation problem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem/We%20prove%20next%20that%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20%24S%24%20is%20the%20solution%20to%20the%20approximation%20problem%20stated%20at%20the%20beginning%20of%20this%20section.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem/We%20wish%20to%20consider%20the%20following%20type%20of%20approximation%20problem%3A%20Given%20an%20element%20x%20in%20V%2C%20to%20determine%20an%20element%20in%20S%20whose%20distance%20from%20x%20is%20as%20small%20as%20possible.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Best approximation problem in a Euclidean space"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "欧几里得空间中的最佳逼近问题"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Abest-approximation-problem> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Given a Euclidean space V, a finite-dimensional subspace S, and an element x in V, determine the element of S whose distance from x is as small as possible. Section 15.14 sets up this problem, illustrates it by a plane in V_3, and announces that the projection of x on S is its solution." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements/The%20distance%20between%20two%20elements%20x%20and%20y%20is%20defined%20to%20be%20the%20norm%20%24%5C%7Cx%20-%20y%5C%7C%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Distance between two elements of a Euclidean space"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "欧几里得空间中两元素间的距离"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Adistance-between-elements> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "The distance between two elements x and y is defined to be the norm ||x - y||. This turns the norm of 15.10 into the quantity minimized in the approximation problem." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "S perpendicular" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement/If%20S%20is%20a%20plane%20through%20the%20origin%2C%20as%20shown%20in%20Figure%2015.2%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20line%20through%20the%20origin%20perpendicular%20to%20this%20plane.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement/In%20case%20S%20is%20a%20subspace%2C%20then%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20called%20the%20orthogonal%20complement%20of%20S.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthogonal complement of a subspace"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "子空间的正交补"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "When S is a subspace of a Euclidean space V, the subspace S perp of all elements orthogonal to S is called the orthogonal complement of S. Geometrically, the orthogonal complement of a plane through the origin in V_3 is the line through the origin perpendicular to it." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace/It%20is%20a%20simple%20exercise%20to%20verify%20that%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20is%20a%20subspace%20of%20V%2C%20whether%20or%20not%20S%20itself%20is%20one.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "S perp is a subspace whether or not S is one"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "S 垂直总是子空间(无论 S 本身是否为子空间)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-complement-is-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "For any subset S of a Euclidean space V, the set of all elements orthogonal to S is a subspace of V, even when S itself is not a subspace. Apostol states this and leaves the verification as a simple exercise." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition/First%20we%20prove%20that%20an%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20actually%20exists.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition/Next%20we%20prove%20that%20the%20orthogonal%20decomposition%20%2815.16%29%20is%20unique.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthogonal decomposition of an element relative to a subspace"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "元素相对于子空间的正交分解"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "A representation of an element x as a sum x = s + s perp with s in the subspace S and s perp in S perp. Theorem 15.15 asserts that such a decomposition exists and is unique whenever S is finite-dimensional." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "orthogonal to S" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set/An%20element%20in%20V%20is%20said%20to%20be%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20if%20it%20is%20orthogonal%20to%20every%20element%20of%20S.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set/Let%20S%20be%20a%20subset%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20V.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Element orthogonal to a set"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "与集合正交的元素"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-to-a-set> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "An element of a Euclidean space V is said to be orthogonal to a subset S of V if it is orthogonal to every element of S. This lifts orthogonality between two elements (15.11) to orthogonality between an element and an entire set." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace/Let%20%24S%24%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20%24V%24%20%2C%20and%20let%20%24%5C%7Be_1%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20e_n%5C%7D%24%20be%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20for%20%24S%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace/is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x%24%20on%20the%20subspace%20%24S%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Projection of an element on a subspace"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "元素在子空间上的投影"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.15" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-on-a-subspace> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "If {e_1, ..., e_n} is an orthonormal basis for a finite-dimensional subspace S of a Euclidean space V and x is in V, the element s given by the sum of (x, e_i) e_i is called the projection of x on the subspace S. It is exactly the S-component of the orthogonal decomposition of x, and Theorem 15.16 (in Section 15.15) shows it solves the approximation problem." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "Pythagorean formula" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition/Moreover%2C%20the%20norm%20of%20%24x%24%20is%20given%20by%20the%20Pythagorean%20formula> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition/the%20remaining%20terms%20being%20zero%20since%20%24s%24%20and%20%24s%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20are%20orthogonal.%20This%20proves%20%2815.17%29.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Pythagorean formula for an orthogonal decomposition"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交分解的勾股公式"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Apythagorean-formula-for-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Part of Theorem 15.15: if x = s + s perp is the orthogonal decomposition of x relative to a finite-dimensional subspace S, then the square of the norm of x equals the sum of the squares of the norms of s and s perp, because the cross terms in the expansion of (s + s perp, s + s perp) vanish by orthogonality." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "S perpendicular" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3As-perp-notation/The%20set%20of%20all%20elements%20orthogonal%20to%20S%20is%20denoted%20by%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24%20and%20is%20called> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Notation S perp for the set of all elements orthogonal to S"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "记号 S 垂直(与 S 正交的全体元素)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "notation" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3As-perp-notation> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "The set of all elements orthogonal to a subset S is denoted by S with a perpendicular superscript and is read 'S perpendicular'. The same symbol is used for the orthogonal complement when S is a subspace." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "ORTHOGONAL DECOMPOSITION THEOREM" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "THEOREM 15.15" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition/THEOREM%2015.15.%20ORTHOGONAL%20DECOMPOSITION%20THEOREM.%20Let%20V%20be%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20and%20let%20S%20be%20a%20finite-dimensional%20subspace%20of%20V.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition/Then%20every%20element%20x%20in%20V%20can%20be%20represented%20uniquely%20as%20a%20sum%20of%20two%20elements%2C%20one%20in%20S%20and%20one%20in%20%24S%5E%7B%5Cperp%7D%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthogonal decomposition theorem (Theorem 15.15)"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交分解定理(定理 15.15)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-15-orthogonal-decomposition> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Let V be a Euclidean space and S a finite-dimensional subspace. Then every x in V can be written uniquely as x = s + s perp with s in S and s perp in S perp, and the norm of x obeys the Pythagorean formula. Existence is proved by taking a finite orthonormal basis of S and setting s equal to the sum of the projections of x along the basis elements; uniqueness follows because the zero element is the only element orthogonal to itself." .
